feat(install): carry a userland stream to the writable root over bulk OUT

The kernel can mount userdata as a read-write HFS+ root and can already carry
console commands over bulk OUT, but there is no path that puts a large file
tree on that volume: the payload volume is 64 MiB and the host has no
block-level route to HFS+.  This adds the receiving end of a streamed install
and the console protocol that drives it, so the next stage has something to
boot from instead of nothing to boot.

  - bsd/dev/kebab_install.c parses the KIST stream that
    scripts/pack-userland.py writes: a 32-byte stream header, then fixed
    192-byte entry headers (destination, mode, flags, wire size, file size,
    SHA-256, path) and payloads, then zero padding to a chunk boundary.  The
    parser only ever moves forward, so the host needs no seeking and the
    receiver never has to know the file length in advance.
  - each entry becomes a file under the root: missing parent directories are
    created with namei(CREATE)+vn_create, the file is opened with
    vnode_open(O_CREAT|O_TRUNC) and written with vn_rdwr, and the mode from the
    header is applied with vnode_setattr (vnode_open only honours cmode when it
    creates the file).
  - every entry is verified twice.  The payload is hashed as it arrives, which
    proves the transport, and the finished file is read back and hashed, which
    proves the volume.  A mismatch stops the stream with FAIL rather than
    leaving a wrong file behind as if it were good.
  - resume: a destination that already has the entry's size and SHA-256 prints
    SKIP and its payload is discarded.  INST CHECK <path> reports the same
    size and hash without transferring anything, so the host can also leave
    those entries out of a resumed stream entirely.
  - INST DRY runs the identical parser, hashes everything and prints
    "PLAN <path> <size>" per entry plus a total, but creates no directory and
    writes no byte.  That is what proves the transport without committing
    3.3 GB.
  - destinations are confined to an allowlist of prefixes (/sbin/, /usr/lib/,
    /usr/standalone/, /System/Library/Caches/com.apple.dyld/,
    /System/Library/Caches/com.apple.kernelcaches/,
    /System/Library/LaunchDaemons/) and any path with a ".." component, an
    empty component, a non-printable byte or an over-long name is refused
    before a directory is created.  The whole family is behind kebab_install=1,
    default off.
  - per-entry zlib is supported both ways: the packer's flag bit says the
    payload is one deflate stream, and the receiver inflates it incrementally
    through ki_zalloc/ki_zfree because libkern's zlib has no BSD-caller
    allocator configured.  A 64 MiB slice of shard .01 compresses to 35.1%, so
    deflate is worth having on this link.
  - fail soft throughout: a short read, a failed write, a hash mismatch, an
    inflate error, a chunk larger than the stream or 60 s of silence prints
    INST ABORT/FAIL and returns the console to command mode.  Nothing here
    panics, and every length from the wire is bounds-checked against the bytes
    actually remaining.

The console side changes only what a stream needs.  The DMA arena grows from
one 4 KiB page to a contiguous 68 KiB (verified page by page with
pmap_find_phys, because the chunk path names the whole buffer in one TRB) and
gains a 64 KiB chunk buffer at +0x1000.  Bulk OUT is armed with 512 bytes in
command mode and with the whole chunk while a stream runs, which is what turns
a per-byte console into a transport: at the measured 2.4 ms round trip a
512-byte chunk would move 200 KB/s, and 3.3 GB would take five hours.  A
completion is interpreted by the buffer it was armed with, not by the current
mode, so a mode change cannot make the parser read a command out of the chunk
buffer.  The endpoint is left unarmed while a chunk is being written and while
a command that may change the mode is still pending, so the next arm always has
the right buffer and the host is NAKed rather than overrunning it.  The
installer itself runs on the event thread outside the transport gates, because
vnode I/O can sleep and the console has to keep transmitting while a chunk is
written.

Protocol, one bulk OUT transfer per step, defined in docs/BRING-UP-STATE.md:

    INST BEGIN <wire_bytes>\n    take the stream and write it
    INST DRY <wire_bytes>\n      take the stream, write nothing
    INST?\n                      gate, root read-only, progress
    INST CHECK <path>\n          current size and SHA-256 of a destination
    INST ACK <n>\n               n bytes consumed; the host sends the next chunk

Measured on hardware (boot_b, kebab_install=1, rootrw=1): a dry run over the
whole 3.3 GB plan and a real transfer of one large shard, with the throughput
and the hash verdicts recorded in docs/BRING-UP-STATE.md section 62.
